.TH CHTTPCLI_ENGINE_WAIT 3 "2026-10-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpcli_engine_wait \- block until the async engine of chttpclient has stopped completely
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

void chttpcli_engine_wait(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
chttpclient's async engine serves Tier 2 and Tier 3
(chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_async_streaming,
chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming) for every
client of the process. It starts with the first request of those tiers
and stops once no client holds it: the last such request has
ended and the last idle connection of those tiers is closed, which
chttpclient_destroy does for its own client. The engine's teardown then
runs on a separate thread, after chttpclient_destroy has returned.

This function blocks until that teardown is over and joins the thread
that ran it. Once it returns, no engine thread runs, and the engine
makes no further call to the allocator that
chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs installed or through the logger
that chttpcli_set_engine_logger installed, so the application may
release both. If no engine runs, the function returns at once.

The function waits as long as any client holds the engine: a client
that is not destroyed and keeps idle Tier 2 or Tier 3 connections, a
request of those tiers that is in flight, or the default client of
chttp_default_client once it has used those tiers. The library's exit
handler destroys the default client; destroy every other such client
before you call this function.

fork(2) waits for the same teardown on its own, so a process that
destroys its last client and then forks does not need to call this
function. The exception is a build with CCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0,
where fork(2) does not wait and the call must come before fork(2).

A call from an engine thread, that is, from the write_fn of
chttpclient_do_async_streaming or chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming,
would wait for its own thread, so it calls _ccol_fatal_err() instead.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Any number of threads may call this function at the same time, and
each call returns once the engine has stopped.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

static ccol_memmgmt_procs_t engine_mp; /* filled by the application */

int main(void) {
  chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(&engine_mp);
  chttpcli cli = chttpclient_create(NULL);
  chttp_request_t *req =
      chttp_request_create(CHTTP_GET, "http://example.com/", NULL, NULL);
  chttpcli_response *resp = NULL;
  if (req && chttpclient_do_pooled(cli, req, &resp) == ccol_success)
    chttpclient_resp_free(resp);
  chttp_request_free(req);
  chttpclient_destroy(cli);
  /* The engine stops on a thread of its own; wait for it before the
   * allocator of the engine goes away. */
  chttpcli_engine_wait();
  return 0;
}
.fi
